1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,720
A trucker reports check-in took forty
seconds. Gateway logs look fine. Gate logs
show a slow billing call. Billing logs
show a slow DB. Without shared
identifiers, those three files are three
novels.

2
00:00:15,839 --> 00:00:23,951
Distributed tracing stitches them: one
trace id for the whole check-in, one span
per hop, parent-child links across gateway
→ gate → billing.

3
00:00:24,071 --> 00:00:31,367
In modern Spring, Micrometer Tracing with
an OpenTelemetry or Brave bridge
propagates context over HTTP.

4
00:00:31,487 --> 00:00:41,567
Boot instruments servlet/WebFlux requests
and outbound clients — RestTemplate,
WebClient, Feign — so W3C traceparent (or
B3) headers ride along.

5
00:00:41,688 --> 00:00:47,280
You rarely hand-roll headers; you verify
they appear and that your sampler is
intentional.

6
00:00:47,400 --> 00:01:00,480
When a hop is missing from the waterfall,
the usual culprit is an outbound client
built without the instrumented builder — a
plain WebClient.create() that never
inherited the observation filter. Look at
this example.

7
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:13,392
# gate-service & billing-service Look at
this java for a moment. Follow one check-
in. Gateway creates root span http POST
/api/gates/G12/check-ins.

8
00:01:13,512 --> 00:01:25,968
Gate continues the trace on inbound
because traceparent arrived, adds
gate.accept, Feign starts a child span for
GET billing-service/tariffs/quote.
Billing’s inbound span nests under that
child.

9
00:01:26,087 --> 00:01:33,815
In a trace UI you see where the forty
seconds went — connection wait, SQL, or a
circuit-open fail-fast that returns in
milliseconds.

10
00:01:33,935 --> 00:01:40,919
Breaker opens that finish in 2ms look
different from 8s timeouts; traces make
that visible without guessing.

11
00:01:41,039 --> 00:01:51,791
Log lines carrying %X{traceId} let you
jump from a booth complaint ticket to the
exact waterfall when the trucker gives you
a time window. Sampling is policy.

12
00:01:51,911 --> 00:02:00,743
probability: 1.0 is fine for a staging
quay; production usually samples a
fraction and always keeps error traces if
your stack supports it.

13
00:02:00,863 --> 00:02:15,479
High-cardinality tags — raw truck plate on
every span — explode storage the same way
metric tags do. Prefer low-cardinality
dimensions (gate.id, hazard.class) and put
unique identifiers in logs correlated by
trace id.

14
00:02:15,599 --> 00:02:24,335
Failure symptoms without tracing
discipline: three teams each swear their
service was fine; MTTR stretches while
someone greps by approximate timestamp;

15
00:02:24,455 --> 00:02:29,831
a missing child span makes billing look
instantaneous when Feign was never
instrumented.

16
00:02:29,951 --> 00:02:43,776
With tracing but bad clocks, skew across
pods confuses duration math — keep NTP
healthy. With 100% sampling on a hot
gateway, the collector lags and operators
lose the tool during the incident they
need it for.

17
00:02:43,895 --> 00:02:50,639
Trade-offs: full tracing everywhere is
expensive; metrics alone cannot show
critical path across processes.

18
00:02:50,759 --> 00:03:01,055
Start with ingress + inter-service HTTP,
add custom spans where business methods
hide time (gate.accept, ledger.record),
and leave getter noise out of the
waterfall.

19
00:03:01,175 --> 00:03:08,519
Baggage can carry a check-in correlation
across hops, but treat it as carefully as
headers — do not put secrets in baggage.

20
00:03:08,639 --> 00:03:19,127
A misconception is equating “we added a
correlation id filter years ago” with full
distributed tracing; a log field without
parent/child spans does not give you a
critical path.

21
00:03:19,247 --> 00:03:31,679
Another is enabling 100% sampling on a hot
gateway and surprising the collector
budget. A third is forgetting outbound
Feign instrumentation and wondering why
gate spans never show billing children.

22
00:03:31,799 --> 00:03:44,447
Traces explain one request. Resilience
still needs more than open/closed: retries
with backoff, rate limits against a flaky
tide vendor, bulkheads so tide threads
cannot starve gate’s billing pool.

23
00:03:44,567 --> 00:03:49,511
That toolbox is Resilience4j beyond the
single breaker annotation.
